\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Clifford symbols: zero blocks and local volume elements}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{On the \vocabk{simplicial momentum grid}{fdip-013X} take a nonzero
finite-dimensional complex Hermitian Clifford module #{S} with
self-adjoint Euclidean generators #{\varepsilon_j^S}, and a
nonzero constant Hermitian space #{F} of finite dimension.
Let the translation-invariant #{D} on
#{\ell^2(\Lambda)\otimes S\otimes F} have Fourier blocks
##{D(p)=\ui\sum_{j=1}^n f_j(r)(\varepsilon_j^S\otimes\bbI_F)+m(r)\bbI,}
where #{f:R\to\mathbb R^n}, #{m:R\to\mathbb R} and
#{p_j=2\pi r_j/N_j}. Then
##{D(p)^\dagger D(p)=(|f(r)|^2+m(r)^2)\bbI.}
Thus a block vanishes exactly when #{f(r)=0=m(r)}, and every
other block is invertible. Suppose a site-independent complex-linear
self-adjoint involution #{\Gamma} on #{S\otimes F} anticommutes
with every #{\varepsilon_j^S\otimes\bbI_F}. Then in every
dimension #{\Gamma D(p)+D(p)\Gamma=2m(r)\Gamma}.
The spin grading of the [massive graded Wilson data](fdip-00IN)
extends by the identity on #{F} to give such an involution.
In even dimension the volume grading #{C} of \ref{fdip-009X}
also satisfies this hypothesis.}

\p{At a vertex zero #{r}, suppose there is one invertible real
linear map #{J} such that for every simplex containing #{r}
and every vertex #{r+w} of that simplex, #{f(r+w)=Jw}.
Here #{w} is the offset in the local grid coordinates at #{r}.
Then #{r} is a \vocabk{nondegenerate vertex zero}{fdip-013Y}
with #{\deg_r f=\operatorname{sgn}\det J}. In the generating
vector space put #{\varepsilon'_h=\sum_jJ_{jh}\varepsilon_j}.
Before representing the Clifford algebra on #{S}, one has
##{\varepsilon'_1\wedge\cdots\wedge\varepsilon'_n
=(\det J)\,\varepsilon_1\wedge\cdots\wedge\varepsilon_n.}
The local degree records the orientation of this volume.
In geometric algebra the product of two vectors contains both
their inner and exterior products. For the #{n} transformed
vectors, taking the top-grade part of their product extracts
the oriented volume, a multiple of #{\psI}.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{A=\sum_j f_j(r)(\varepsilon_j^S\otimes\bbI_F)}.
Self-adjointness and the Clifford relations give #{A^\dagger=A}
and #{A^2=|f(r)|^2\bbI}. Multiplying #{m\bbI-\ui A} by
#{m\bbI+\ui A} gives the block formula. A positive scalar on
the right gives an inverse by dividing #{D(p)^\dagger} by that
scalar. The supplied #{\Gamma} anticommutes with each term of #{A}
and commutes with the real mass, giving the stated anticommutator.}

\p{In a lifted unit cube the simplex with steps in order #{\pi}
is the region #{1\ge x_{\pi(1)}\ge\cdots\ge x_{\pi(n)}\ge0}.
The successive differences in this chain of inequalities
are its barycentric weights. Sorting coordinates covers each
cube, with ties on common faces. These regions match across
cube faces. Hence the cones based at #{r} cover every direction,
and a direction outside their proper faces belongs to exactly
one cone. The map #{J} preserves this property and independence.
In the ordered simplex the edge determinant is
#{\operatorname{sgn}(\pi)}, since successive column subtractions
give the permuted unit vectors. Applying #{J} multiplies that
determinant by #{\det J}. The signed contribution in the unique
selected cone is therefore #{\operatorname{sgn}\det J}. Expanding
the exterior product by multilinearity, repeated indices vanish
and the permutations contribute their signs. Their coefficient
sum is #{\det J}.}
}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{exteriorPower.ιMultiDual_apply_ιMulti}
computes the basis coordinate of a wedge by the determinant of
the corresponding coordinate matrix.}
